course: history
lesson: 1
title: The first people: hunters, gatherers and cave painters
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: For almost all of the time humans have existed, nobody farmed, built towns or wrote anything down: people lived by hunting animals and gathering plants.
explain: The first people like us lived in Africa about 300,000 years ago, and over many thousands of years their children's children walked out and spread over the whole world.
explain: Picture a camping trip with no tent from a shop, no matches and no supermarket: you must know which berries are safe, where the deer drink and how to keep a fire alive all night. Hunter-gatherers knew all that by heart, along with hundreds of plants and animals.
explain: They were just as clever as us. They made sharp stone tools and bone needles, sewed fur clothes in the Ice Age, told stories round the fire and painted horses and bulls on cave walls.
explain: Cartoon cavemen who grunt and ride dinosaurs are pure fantasy: dinosaurs died out about 66 million years before the first humans.
fact: The first humans like us, Homo sapiens, lived in Africa about 300,000 years ago, and every person alive today has ancestors from Africa.
fact: From Africa people spread step by step over the whole world over many tens of thousands of years; they did not appear everywhere at once.
fact: For most of human history people were hunter-gatherers who hunted, fished and gathered berries, nuts and roots, moving camp with the seasons.
fact: Hunter-gatherers lived in small bands of a few families who shared food and looked after each other.
fact: The earliest tools were stones chipped to a sharp edge; later people also made fine blades, spear points, bone needles and fish hooks, which is why this long time is called the Stone Age.
fact: People learned to keep and use fire hundreds of thousands of years ago, which gave warmth, light, protection from animals and cooked food.
fact: During the last Ice Age huge sheets of ice covered the north, and people lived alongside woolly mammoths, cave bears and giant deer.
fact: Dinosaurs died out about 66 million years ago, millions of years before any humans existed.
fact: In caves such as Lascaux in France and Altamira in Spain people painted horses, bulls and deer with colours made from earth and charcoal, and some cave paintings are over 30,000 years old.
fact: People also blew paint around their hands to leave hand outlines, called stencils, which are found all over the world, including Indonesia and Argentina.
fact: Nobody knows exactly when language began, but early humans talked, sang and told stories.
fact: Ötzi the Iceman lived about 5,300 years ago; his body was kept by the ice of the Alps and found by hikers in 1991, with his copper axe, bow and grass-stuffed shoes.
exercise: On which continent did the first people like us live? | Africa | Southern Europe | East Asia | Australia
exercise: About how long ago did the first humans like us live? | About 300,000 years ago | About 3,000 years ago | About 66 million years ago | About 30 years ago
exercise: How did people spread over the world? | Step by step over many thousands of years | All at once, on the very same day everywhere | By sailing big ships from Europe | They appeared separately everywhere
exercise: How did hunter-gatherers get their food? | By hunting, fishing and gathering wild plants | By farming wheat in big fields round their villages | By buying it at markets in towns | By keeping cows in barns
exercise: Why did hunter-gatherers not build big stone houses? | They moved camp with the seasons | They did not know what stone was | Stone was forbidden by their chiefs | They always lived in trees instead
exercise: What were the earliest tools made of? | Stone chipped to a sharp edge | Iron hammered into shape | Bronze poured into moulds | Plastic and wood glued together
exercise: Why is this long early time called the Stone Age? | Because the best tools were made of stone | Because people lived inside stones | Because the weather was stony, grey and cold | Because people ate small stones to stay strong
exercise: Which of these did fire NOT do for the first people? | Keep their food cold for weeks | Give warmth on cold nights | Scare away wild animals | Cook food so it was easier to eat
exercise: Which animal lived at the same time as Ice Age people? | The woolly mammoth | Tyrannosaurus rex | The giant diplodocus | The flying pterodactyl
exercise: What did Ice Age people use to sew fur clothes? | Needles made of bone | Needles made of steel | Little sewing machines | Pins made of pure gold
exercise: Where are the famous cave paintings of Lascaux? | In France | In Egypt | In northern China | In Brazil's jungle
exercise: What did cave painters use for colours? | Earth and charcoal | Felt pens and crayons | Oil paint from tubes | Blue dye from the sea
exercise: Where have hand stencils on cave walls been found? | All over the world, such as Indonesia and Argentina | Only in one cave in France, near the village of Lascaux | Only in Africa | Only in the far north
exercise: How did hunter-gatherers usually live? | In small bands of a few families | In big cities of many thousands of people | Each person all alone | In stone castles ruled by a king
exercise: Who found Ötzi the Iceman, and when? | Hikers in the Alps in 1991 | Farmers in Egypt in 1822 | Sailors near Iceland in 1066 | Soldiers in Russia in 1812
exercise: What did Ötzi carry with him? | A copper axe and a bow | A steel sword and a shield | A clay tablet with writing | A bronze helmet with horns
test: my little bro says cavemen rode dinosaurs, he saw it on telly. right? | No, dinosaurs died out millions of years before humans | Yes, but only small dinosaurs | Yes, in Africa they did | No, they rode woolly mammoths everywhere
test: A cartoon shows cavemen as grunting and stupid. What is closer to the truth? | They were as clever as us and knew hundreds of plants | They really could not talk at all | They were cleverer than anyone alive today | They only knew how to hunt and nothing else
test: Your mate says people appeared in every country at the same moment. Is he right? | No, people spread out of Africa step by step | Yes, people appeared everywhere at once, in every land | No, they all came from Europe first | Yes, but only after farming began
test: If a hunter-gatherer band had no fire in the Ice Age, what would be hardest? | Keeping warm through the freezing nights | Finding enough sand to play in by the river | Keeping their pots clean | Writing down their stories in books
test: Why are hands painted on cave walls in Indonesia and Argentina so interesting? | People far apart had the same idea | They prove both places were one country | They were painted by the same person | They show people wrote letters to each other
test: You must move camp every few weeks. What would you own? | Only what you could carry | A heavy stone oven | A big library of books | A herd of cows kept in a barn
test: Ötzi's shoes were stuffed with grass. What does that tell us? | People were clever about staying warm | Ötzi had no feet | Grass was used as money in his day | Shoes were not yet invented in his time
test: Why do we know a lot about Ötzi but little about most people of his time? | The ice kept his body and his things | He wrote a long diary | He was a famous king | Someone painted his whole life in a cave
test: Bloody hell, how old are them cave paintings at Lascaux and Altamira? | Some paintings in such caves are over 30,000 years old | About 300 years old | About 3 million years old, before people | Made last century as a joke by some local kids
test: Which is true about when people started talking? | Nobody knows exactly when language began | Language began exactly 5,000 years ago, in Egypt | People only grunted until farming began | Talking was invented by the Romans
test: Why would a band of hunter-gatherers share their food? | They looked after each other to survive | Keeping food for yourself was against the law | They had too much to ever eat | Sharing was forced by a king
exam: Identify the continent which is established as the original home of anatomically modern humans. | Africa | Europe | South America | Antarctica
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the mode of subsistence of humankind for the greater part of its history? | Hunting, fishing and the gathering of wild plants | The cultivation of cereals in permanent fields | The purchase of provisions in urban markets | The rearing of cattle in enclosed barns
exam: It is asserted in certain animated productions that early humans coexisted with dinosaurs. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The assertion is false, the dinosaurs having perished some 66 million years earlier | The assertion is accurate in respect of the smaller dinosaurs | The assertion is accurate in respect of Africa alone | The assertion is accurate in respect of the Ice Age
exam: Ascertain the approximate antiquity of the body designated Ötzi, recovered from the Alpine ice in 1991. | Approximately 5,300 years | Approximately 530 years | Approximately 53,000 years | Approximately 5.3 million years
